  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Blazing Here Comes Sunshine jam fuels epic Eyes of the World and tight segues",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a scorching Here Comes Sunshine jam that launches into a marathon Eyes Of The World. Jerry’s lead work is on fire, while the band strings together seamless transitions – Weather Report Suite Prelude into Eyes and China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider. Strong vocal harmonies on Box of Rain and a punchy encore round out an all‑around stellar performance.",
    "review": "Boston Garden on April 2, 1973 captured one of the spring ’73 high points for the Dead. The first set rolls through tight rockers – The Promised Land, Deal, Brown Eyed Women – before building momentum with Wave That Flag and a soulful Box of Rain featuring Donna’s soaring backing vocals. Set two ignites with a ripping Here Comes Sunshine that spills into an expansive Eyes Of The World, highlighted by Jerry’s “tear‑through” solos and Phil’s rock‑solid bass foundation. The Weather Report Suite Prelude slides effortlessly into the same marathon jam, then bursts into a crisp China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition famed for its “Mind Left Body Jam.” Highlights continue with Playing In The Band, a fluid Greatest Story Ever Told, and a bright Sugar Magnolia before the band closes with Johnny B. Goode and a heartfelt And We Bid You Good Night.\n\nAcross all five soundboard sources the performance feels locked in – Jerry is \"very on all night,\" delivering soaring leads; Phil anchors every groove; Keith’s keyboards weave smoothly through the extended improvisations; Bill (and Mickey, when present) keep a propulsive beat. The Miller‑remastered version shines with crisp clarity, but the yerys capture remains the recommended listen for its clear mix and minimal bleed, letting every nuance of this legendary night ring true.",
